+- **What loads, what does not.** Verified on this host (Claude Code 2.1.293) with a `claude --bg` canary carrying the skills-lane argv (round 1's, before the plugin `Write` deny was added; the deny only tightens what the session may write, not what loads): the session loads the pinned plugin's skills (`workflow-<lane>:<name>`) and Claude Code's own bundled skills (on 2.1.293: `dataviz`, `artifact-design`, `artifact-diagramming`, `artifact-capabilities`, `update-config`, `keybindings-help`, `code-review`, `simplify`, `fewer-permission-prompts`, `loop`, `schedule`, `claude-api`, `workflow-authoring`, `run`, `plugin-authoring`, `init`, `security-review`), and **no** user skill from `~/.claude/skills/`. It loads **no** operator hooks and **no** user MCP servers (the canary reported `HOOKS: no`, `MCP: no`), and the deny list in `--settings` still applies (a `Read` of a denied path is refused). The `Skill` tool and the advisor are both present in its tools. Auto-memory, keychain reads and background prefetches are Claude Code's own; a skills lane neither enables nor disables them beyond what `--setting-sources ""` does.
 - **CLAUDE.md.** No session gets the operator-notes section of the worktree's `CLAUDE.md` — a skills lane no more than a `--safe-mode` lane. The canary ran in a scratch repository whose `CLAUDE.md` held a unique token below its operator-notes heading and reported `TOKENS: NONE` and that no `CLAUDE.md` / operator-notes section was in its context, the same answer a `--safe-mode` session gives. The controller pins the project's conventions above that heading into the prompt, as for every lane.
 - **The advisor and the lost settings keys.** The operator's `~/.claude/settings.json` applies to a `--safe-mode` lane, so the advisor is already there; under `--setting-sources ""` it is gone, and the controller restores only `advisorModel`, read once at prepare and pinned as `plan.nodes[<lane>].advisor_model` (string or null; every launch and `launch --dry-run` read the plan, never the file). A skills lane loses every other key of the operator's settings file (its model default, permissions and hooks): accepted, since the model, effort, permissions and deny list come from the controller.
-- **The pinned plugin.** At prepare the controller copies each named `~/.claude/skills/<name>/` (symlinks resolved, modes preserved, `.git` skipped) into `<run>/skills/<lane>/skills/<name>/` and writes `<run>/skills/<lane>/.claude-plugin/plugin.json` (`{"name": "workflow-<lane>", ...}`). The copy is run evidence like the prompt, so a later edit of the operator's skill folder changes no running run. Two digests: `plan.nodes[<lane>].skills[].sha256` is the digest of that skill's own subtree (its sorted relative paths and contents, so the same skill text gives the same digest in two runs), and `plan.worker_authority.skills_sha256[<lane>]` is the digest of the whole plugin tree (which includes the run id in `plugin.json`, so it changes per run). `worker_authority.skills_settings_sha256[<lane>]` is the digest of that lane's skills `--settings` variant; `worker_settings_sha256` stays the digest of the plain worker settings. The lane's `--settings` adds one deny, `Edit(/<run>/skills/<lane>/**)`, so a session cannot alter its own pinned plugin (only the Edit rule family is denied: a `Write(...)` rule never matches under Claude Code's permission engine). A skill's own launcher binary is the session's business (the operator fetches it before launch); the controller neither fetches nor installs anything.
+- **The pinned plugin.** At prepare the controller copies each named `~/.claude/skills/<name>/` (symlinks resolved, modes preserved, `.git` skipped) into `<run>/skills/<lane>/skills/<name>/` and writes `<run>/skills/<lane>/.claude-plugin/plugin.json` (`{"name": "workflow-<lane>", ...}`). The copy is run evidence like the prompt, so a later edit of the operator's skill folder changes no running run. Two digests: `plan.nodes[<lane>].skills[].sha256` is the digest of that skill's own subtree (its sorted relative paths and contents, so the same skill text gives the same digest in two runs), and `plan.worker_authority.skills_sha256[<lane>]` is the digest of the whole plugin tree (which includes the run id in `plugin.json`, so it changes per run). `worker_authority.skills_settings_sha256[<lane>]` is the digest of that lane's skills `--settings` variant; `worker_settings_sha256` stays the digest of the plain worker settings. The lane's `--settings` adds two denies, `Edit(/<run>/skills/<lane>/**)` and `Write(/<run>/skills/<lane>/**)`, so a session cannot alter its own pinned plugin (decisions.md [L1] g); nothing is removed from the deny list. A skill's own launcher binary is the session's business (the operator fetches it before launch); the controller neither fetches nor installs anything.
 - **A skill's frontmatter.** A `SKILL.md` whose frontmatter declares `hooks` is refused at prepare (naming the lane and the skill). `allowed-tools` in a frontmatter is allowed: a pinned skill may carry it. A skill's `model` frontmatter overrides the lane's pinned model while that skill runs.
 - **Refusals (nothing written, each naming the lane and the skill).** `skills` on a version before 2.8.0; a name that does not match the pattern or is listed twice; a name that does not resolve to `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`; a `SKILL.md` that is not a regular file; a `hooks` frontmatter; a file over 2 MB; a skill folder over 8 MB in total; a symlink that resolves outside the skill folder or forms a loop. `launch` and `launch --dry-run` resolve and check the skills before any Git action; `prepare` checks them again before the run directory exists. The print transport cannot load skills, so a skills lane on it is refused.
 
